cks that angle in the topology of its curvature. \\n\\n\\n\\nWhat's more... we made a list of other things that track with motion. Circular motion is an orbit, and that sweeps out a helix in time. Heat is a jitter in the line. Elastic collision is two lines approaching, making contact, and then abruptly changing direction. What else? Well... let's strip down to basics, starting with energy. an object at rest is a straight line perpendicular to the timesheet An object in motion is a straight line at an angle. The worldline of an object with greater mass *curves less sharply* when subjected to a force. These follow from the diagram logic itself, not inserted. Put a pin in this.\\n\\n\\n\\nNow... what are we going to do about internal degrees of freedom of a particle?  We have two primitives--the worldline and the timesheet. Obviously, particle properties live on the worldline more naturally than on the timesheet. So, we return to the infinite representation capacity of the curve, and we note that these properties are already routinely represented in a way that naturally fits on a curve equation--oscillations. So, we allow the small leap: the curve equation of a worldline must oscillate. Which is to say, that it must be helical. That buys us some options. The intersection with a 3D 'plane' of a helix, extended along the 4-dimensional could be interpreted multiple ways; a simple helix would likely produce a circular trace, and our choice of braiding as the grammar for color confinement gives us two additional intersectional geometries: something like a borromean knot for a hadron, and something like a hopf link for a meson. So, we've got the strong force and color charge already. So, we need spin and flavor. Let us tentatively decide to encode these as nested superhelical coils.   \\n\\n\\n\\nNow, we don't really have to do that arbitrarily; string theory kind of does that for us, at least in part--because if you look down the barrel of a helix, you see that the intersectional geometry could very easily be represented as an ST vibration mode--so, we just have to collapse ST down to the three-dimensional slice that constitutes our slice. \\n\\n\\n\\nNow, we've made three geometrically motivated leaps of logic or represenational choices. We have one more to make. We need to decide what is going on at the intersection. Is the timesheet just passively truncating a portion of the 4D worldline that constitutes the particle that we see? Or is there some *action* here? Let's think about this--if it's passive, then it's a pure formalism, and it cannot have any physical reality; but by the grammar of the diagram, we've already committed to this truncation somehow *instantiating* a particle. Therefore, it *must* be an active boundary. We've already identified, by the logic of the geometry, that geometric parameters... namely, the intersection angle... are associated with velocity, mass, inertia... so let us propose our linchpin: If angle encodes mass (and by f=ma, it must encode force and acceleration as well), then by E=mc^2, that intersection must also encode somehow energy and the speed of light. By the logic of the diagram, the speed of light is already in the motion of the timesheet; thus, c is the speed of *time*.  And there is an energy component associated with the angle, so let us propose that the timesheet exerts a force--that is to say, an energy transfer--upon the worldline, proportional to angle of intersection. This is quite intuitive, as we can think of it almost like friction; a perfectly perpendicular worldline (or a worldine perfectly parallel to the direction of propogation) offers minimum resistance, and thus minimum energy transfer; so, let's measure that angle against the time normal, and say zero degrees is zero energy transfer, and as a worldline approaches 90 degrees (that is, parallel to the timesheet, and perpendicular to the direction of motion), it offers more resistance and picks up more energy.\\n\\n\\n\\nIn this picture, mass is essentially 'time drag'. The long axis of a helical worldline would be the motion vector, but the actual point contact would be the pitch of the helix, or rather (since we're measuring from time normal), the inverse pitch. So, tentatively we can say the actual intersectional angle of particle worldlines approaches 90, and the corresponding resistance is approximately equated to some minimal mass unit--we're going to choose the mass of the electron for reasons I'll get to shortly. \\n\\n\\n\\nNow implicit in this picture, we have to consider the fact that we have particles that move at the speed of light, or rather, in our picture, they move at the speed of time... that is, they move with the timesheet, and our energy transfer mechanism makes this natural to interpret as the 'push' of the timesheet. Eg, photons ride worldlines, pushed along by time. This doesn't sound like an extended helical coil necessarily. This sounds like a small excitation. So, let us propose this: A photon is a deviation in a worldline, instantiated as an angular distortion, and carried along as a single oscillation or small packet of oscillations. That fits pretty well. But if that's the case, then other particles that travel at c should be structurally similar. So, now consider what the neutrino might look like in this picture. The neutrino has a measured mass. But if the timesheet exerts pressure upon the kink in the worldline, then by Newton, there must be an equal and opposite force placed upon the timesheet by the particle's kink as well. So, that explains neutrino mass as inertial back-reaction, and the photon must also have such inertial mass, however small. But otherwise they, and other 'massless' particles would seem to fall into a family. Massive particles would be associated with a helical coil, extended long the time axis, while massless particles are small, mobile oscillations pushed by the timesheet. [Put a big pin right here.]\\n\\n\\n\\nSo... let us propose our sixth leap of logic, still strongly geometrically motivated, but now including our energy transfer picture: If the timesheet exerts a force upon the 'kink surface' of the excitation that constitutes our photon or neutrino, propagating that distortion, and that kink has a Newtonian normal force upon the time sheet... then the time sheet--which we've already argued must be a real, active participant--ought to record that force in some way as well. Using the simplest option for how we might represent this, adding the least novel modification to our grammar, we decide that, like the distortion of the worldline, this force manifests as distortion of the timesheet. And let us tentatively suppose then, that a perfectly straight worldline, oriented directly along the axis of time, 0 degrees to the propagation and 90 degrees to the surface, minimal interaction....  that sounds like vacuum. And if our particles are helices, then their local intersection angles are presumably towards the high end, which gives us a lot of room for slightly tilted worldlines to carry--or rather to have imparted upon them--vacuum energy. \\n\\n\\n\\nNow, think about that. By trying to follow the minimal logic of Minkowski's existing worldline-timesheet formalism, without manufacturing a single equation, and trying to carefully track our logical leaps so that they are the minimum allowed by the geometric grammar, we've used a system that we *know* is empirically justified and worked through some of the implications of asking that grammar to encode as much of the physical picture as it can. Almost effortlessly, it gives us a mechanism for baryogenesis (or at least mass generation, the genesis of the coiling itself will have to wait), it's given us a mechanism for bosogenesis (or at least 'bosopropagation' in our photon mechanism), it already gives us acceleration and momentum and inertia by construction, it gave us, with just a tiny bit of creativity, color confinement, the strong force (with a very convincing explanation for its strength...if you're trying to pull apart braided worldlines, you're working against the worldlines themselves [implicit in the SAT proposition is that worldlines' physical materiality is extensive across time, but had we not assumed that, then it would have arisen naturally, and necessarily from the braiding proposition]). \\n\\n\\n\\nAnd now, we've constructed by tweak alone, without aiming for it at all, a system where the distortion of worldlines (mass) induces a distortion of the timesheet. And *that*, accidentally lands us exactly where Minkowski started. We have a precise mirror of the metric. Only... it's on the timesheet. But if you think about it... generations of physics students have thought, how weird is it that in GR that mass and motion and space and time are all tangled up. But here we find where GR *actually* lives in the Minkowski model, naturally. If mass distorts the time sheet (literally stretching it against its direction of motion)... then of *course* mass would slow time--the interaction implicit in the Minkowski diagram is already an interaction between mass and *time*, not mass and space. Of course, we've committed to bringing standard science along for the ride... so we recall that space and time *are the same thing*. But let's take that seriously, and since we're minimalists, and we've already made, six I believe, assumptions; I feel like we should reduce our assumption count by saying actually, the minus sign in the Lorenz signature is introducing an unnecessary (and problematic, if you've ever done a Wick rotation) asymmetry. Let us adopt spacetime consistency, call it (++++), assume all of the differences between time and space are unnecessary assumptions except for the one that we can actually witness: It moves differently. Specifically, it has a speed of c and because the 'motion' of space is expansion, then expansion is also the motion of time. Suddenly, time and space *do* look nearly identical. Spacetime is a 4-dimensional manifold, expanding along all axes, mostly at H_0, but at H_0 + c along one axis. \\n\\n\\n\\nI *think* we've dramatically improved upon the economy of assumptions for our model over LCDM... and... over the standard model, QCD, ST, and GR. While uniting them in an intuitive and geometrically logical way. In... what? Twelve, fifteen paragraphs without an equation in sight. And you don't really need an equation to grasp the logi"
]

